right-handed-neutrino-mass-vanishing-lim.interpretation:
The limit $M_R ightarrow 0$ is a critical boundary condition for theories of neutrino mass. In the context of the Type-I seesaw mechanism, where the light neutrino mass $m_{
u}$ is given by $m_{
u} \approx -m_D^2 / M_R$, this limit would imply an infinite mass for the light neutrinos ($m_{
u} ightarrow \infty$), which is experimentally falsified. Therefore, if $M_R$ is indeed very small or zero, the seesaw mechanism is not the correct explanation for the observed smallness of $m_{
u}$. In the specific case where $M_R = 0$, the right-handed neutrino $
u_R$ and its left-handed counterpart $
u_L$ would combine to form a Dirac fermion, conserving total lepton number. This would necessitate an alternative mechanism to generate the small but non-zero masses of the observed neutrinos.

right-handed-neutrino-mass-vanishing-lim.limits_and_boundary:
This is a boundary condition for neutrino mass models. In the Type-I seesaw model ($m_{
u} \approx -m_D^2 / M_R$), as $M_R ightarrow 0$, the predicted light neutrino mass $m_{
u}$ diverges to infinity ($m_{
u} ightarrow \infty$), contradicting experimental observations. The opposite limit, $M_R ightarrow \infty$, leads to the desired outcome of $m_{
u} ightarrow 0$. If the Dirac mass term $m_D ightarrow 0$, the light and heavy neutrinos decouple, and the light neutrino mass $m_{
u}$ would also approach zero, independent of the value of $M_R$.
